---
title: Defining a command handler
description: Writing the one handler that serves a command, and dispatching it.
---

A command has exactly one handler. It is an ordinary class — no base class, no registration —
and the generator finds it through the contract it implements.

## With a result

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

## With no result

```csharp
public sealed class ArchiveOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<ArchiveOrder>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(ArchiveOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => await repository.ArchiveAsync(command.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Dependencies

Constructor parameters are resolved from the **dispatching scope**, so a scoped
`IOrderRepository` behaves exactly as it does in a controller. Nothing special is needed:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(
    IOrderRepository repository,
    IPricingService pricing,
    ILogger<PlaceOrderHandler> logger) : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid> { … }
```

{% aside type="tip" %}
A handler with a single public constructor and plain service parameters can be constructed
directly by a compiled plan, skipping the container entirely. Several public constructors keep
it on the container path and are reported as `ERGO003` — informational, since everything still
works.
{% /aside %}

## Always pass the cancellation token

```csharp
await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
```

This is the most common omission, and it only shows up under load.

## Dispatching

```csharp
public sealed class OrdersController(ICommandMediator mediator)
{
    public async Task<IResult> Post(PlaceOrder command, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command, ct);
        return Results.Created($"/orders/{id}", id);
    }
}
```

The overloads:

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command);                              // no result
await mediator.SendAsync(command, ct);
var id = await mediator.SendAsync(new PlaceOrder(…));           // with result
var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command, GroupSet.Of("audit"), ct);
await mediator.SendAsync(command, context);                     // share a context
```

When both the message and result types are known at the call site, naming them lets the
dispatch build its executor from arguments the compiler already resolved rather than
constructing it reflectively:

```csharp
var id = await mediator.SendAsync<PlaceOrder, Guid>(command, ct);
```

That matters for NativeAOT rather than for throughput — the two paths measure within a
nanosecond of each other.

## Covariant handlers

A handler registered against a base type serves every command in that family:

```csharp
public sealed class OrderCommandHandler : ICommandHandler<OrderCommand> { … }
```

A **direct** handler always wins over a covariant one, with no ambiguity — that is the idiom for
a default with specific overrides:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder> { … }   // wins for PlaceOrder
```

Two handlers at the same level is `ERGO010`, a build error, because the ladder has no
tiebreaker within a level.

## Handlers are not where cross-cutting work goes

Logging, validation, authorization and metrics belong in interceptors — otherwise you write
them again in the next handler. See
[Interceptor or handler](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/interceptor-or-handler).

## Testing one

Construct it, because it is an ordinary class:

```csharp
var handler = new PlaceOrderHandler(new InMemoryOrderRepository());
var id = await handler.HandleAsync(new PlaceOrder(customerId, lines), new ErgosfareContext());
```

That tests the logic. To test that the handler is *reachable* — that its interceptors run and
the dispatch resolves — build a container and dispatch. See
[Composition root and testing](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/composition-root).
